<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guaraní people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Guarani people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Guaraní are an indigenous people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> and neighbouring parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>. Spanish settlers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a> allied with them from the 1530s, taking Guaraní wives and labour, and from 1609 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionaries gathered many of them into mission towns. Their language remains widely spoken in Paraguay.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guarani-people.jpg" alt="Guaraní people" width="320" height="476" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roosewelt Pinheiro/ABr · CC BY 3.0 br · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xam%C3%A3_guarani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
